    example: { given: "Plan the subnet containing 192.0.2.173/26 and verify whether 192.0.2.190 belongs to it.", result: "Network 192.0.2.128, broadcast 192.0.2.191, conventional hosts .129 through .190: 62 hosts.", steps: ["There are 32 - 26 = 6 host bits.", "Total size is 2^6 = 64 addresses.", "Final-octet boundaries are 0, 64, 128, and 192. The address 173 lies in the block starting at 128.", "Its last member is 128 + 64 - 1 = 191.", "Exclude .128 and .191 for conventional host assignment, leaving .129 to .190.", "Masking .190 with 192 gives .128, so it belongs and is the last conventional host.", "For contrast, 192.0.2.10/31 on a supported point-to-point link pairs .10 and .11; neither is discarded as a network or broadcast endpoint."] },
