    steps: ["Start with a safe packet-size assumption and account for all applicable headers.", "For classic IPv4 PMTUD, send with DF and use ICMP fragmentation-needed feedback to reduce size.", "For IPv6, use Packet Too Big feedback; routers do not fragment forwarded packets.", "Packetization-layer discovery also tests sizes and uses delivery confirmation to avoid depending solely on ICMP.", "At ordinary hops, replace link headers; decrement IPv4 TTL and update its checksum, or decrement IPv6 Hop Limit. Preserve endpoint addresses and transport fields unless an explicit function such as NAT changes them."],
    example: { given: "A path has MTUs 1,500, 1,400, and 1,500. A TCP sender uses ordinary 20-byte IPv4 and 20-byte TCP headers, no tunnel.", result: "A 1,400-byte IP packet fits; its maximum TCP data under these headers is 1,360 bytes.", steps: ["Take the minimum link MTU: 1,400.", "A 1,500-byte DF packet fails at the narrow link and should trigger size feedback.", "Reduce IP packet size to 1,400.", "Subtract 20 + 20 header bytes to allow 1,360 data bytes.", "On later routing hops, MAC addresses change and TTL falls, but the TCP data and ordinary endpoint addresses do not need to change."] },
